universe u

/-!
# The projective dimension of the residue field bounds the embedding dimension

Let `(R, 𝔪, κ)` be a Noetherian local ring. Then `dim_κ 𝔪/𝔪² ≤ pd_R κ`, the embedding dimension
of `R` being at most the projective dimension of its residue field.

The inequality is read in `WithBot ℕ∞`, so it is the assertion that `κ` has no projective
resolution shorter than `dim_κ 𝔪/𝔪²`: by `CategoryTheory.projectiveDimension_ge_iff` it is
equivalent to `¬ HasProjectiveDimensionLT κ (dim_κ 𝔪/𝔪²)`, hence to the statement that
`pd_R κ ≤ d` forces `dim_κ 𝔪/𝔪² ≤ d` for every natural number `d`. Since `dim_κ 𝔪/𝔪²` is the
minimal number of generators of `𝔪`
(`IsLocalRing.spanFinrank_maximalIdeal_eq_finrank_cotangentSpace`), what is proved is that
`pd_R κ ≤ d` produces `d` generators of `𝔪`.

The proof is by induction on `d`. Let `pd_R κ ≤ d + 1` and assume `𝔪 ≠ 0`. Prime avoidance
against `𝔪²` and the finitely many associated primes of `R` produces a nonzerodivisor
`x ∈ 𝔪 ∖ 𝔪²`: if there were none, `𝔪` would be an associated prime of `R`, so some `y ≠ 0` would
be annihilated by `𝔪`, and then a finite module of finite projective dimension is free — in
particular `κ` is free, `R` is a field and `𝔪 = 0`. Write `R' = R/xR`. From
`0 → 𝔪 → R → κ → 0` one gets `pd_R 𝔪 ≤ d`, and `x` is a nonzerodivisor on `𝔪`, so a finite free
resolution of `𝔪` over `R` stays exact after `- ⊗_R R'` and
`pd_{R'} 𝔪/x𝔪 ≤ d`. Because `x ∉ 𝔪²`, the submodule `xR/x𝔪 ≅ κ` is a direct summand of `𝔪/x𝔪`,
so `pd_{R'} κ ≤ d`; by induction the maximal ideal `𝔪/xR` of `R'` is generated by `d` elements,
and `𝔪` is generated by those lifted together with `x`.

The two cases `d = 0` and `d = 1` are dispatched without any of this, and unconditionally. For
`d = 0` a projective residue field is finite flat, hence free
(`Module.free_of_flat_of_isLocalRing`), and a free module has trivial annihilator, whereas `𝔪`
annihilates `κ`; so `𝔪 = 0`, the ring is a field and its cotangent space is `0`. For `d = 1` the
exact sequence `0 → 𝔪 → R → κ → 0` makes `𝔪` projective, hence free, and a free ideal of a
commutative ring is principal — two distinct members `a`, `b` of a basis satisfy the relation
`b · a - a · b = 0`, whose coefficients are not both zero — so `dim_κ 𝔪/𝔪² ≤ 1` by
`IsLocalRing.finrank_cotangentSpace_le_one_iff`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.LocalRing.EmbeddingDimension.solution`: the statement of
  `IsLocalRing.finrank_cotangentSpace_le_projectiveDimension_residueField`.

## Implementation notes

The source argues with the Koszul complex `K_•(R, x_•)` on a minimal system of generators of `𝔪`,
lifting `id_κ` to a map of complexes `K_• → F_•` into a minimal finite free resolution of `κ` and
showing by induction that `α_i ⊗ κ : ∧^i κ^n → F_i ⊗ κ` is injective. The route above replaces
that by the descending induction on `d`, which needs no complex beyond a single finite free cover
and no exterior algebra. The two arguments prove the same inequality, and the Koszul one does not
become shorter for having a `κ`-basis of `𝔪/𝔪²` in hand: the minimality of the resolution, which
the source obtains from Lemma 00MT, is exactly what the descending induction obtains from
`x ∉ 𝔪²`.

The four ingredients of the induction are proved here as `private` lemmas, each stated in the
generality it holds in rather than the generality the induction consumes.

* `hasProjectiveDimensionLE_quotSMulTop` is the change of rings `pd_{R/xR} M/xM ≤ pd_R M` for `x`
  a nonzerodivisor of `R` and of a finite module `M`. It is proved by induction on `pd_R M`: a
  finite free cover `0 → N → F → M → 0` has `N ∩ xF = xN`, because `xf ∈ N` gives
  `x · (f mod N) = 0` in `M` and hence `f ∈ N`, so `0 → N/xN → F/xF → M/xM → 0` is exact over
  `R/xR` with `F/xF` finite free. The statement quantifies over the module inside the
  proposition rather than taking `M` as a variable, because the induction changes the module.
* `exists_surjective_forall_mem_maximalIdeal_of_mem_ker` is the existence of a minimal free
  cover of a finite module over a local ring: a surjection `Rⁿ → M` every kernel element of which
  has all coordinates in `𝔪`. It is proved by induction on the size of a generating family, a
  unit coordinate in the kernel being exactly what lets one generator be deleted.
* `free_of_hasProjectiveDimensionLE_of_mul_eq_zero` is Ischebeck's observation that over a local
  ring of depth zero a finite module of finite projective dimension is free. Depth zero is spelled
  by hand, as a nonzero `y` with `𝔪 y = 0`, so no depth theory is needed. The induction is on the
  projective dimension: the kernel `N` of a minimal free cover of `M` is free by induction and
  sits inside `𝔪 Rⁿ`, so `y` kills it, whereas `y` kills no basis vector; hence `N = 0`.
* `exists_isSMulRegular_notMem_sq` is the existence of a nonzerodivisor of `R` in `𝔪 ∖ 𝔪²`. Prime
  avoidance (`Ideal.subset_iUnion_iff_mem_of_isMaximal_of_finite`, which tolerates one non-prime
  member `𝔪²` of the covering family, together with `associatedPrimes.finite`) produces it: were
  `𝔪` covered by `𝔪²` and the associated primes of `R`, it would lie in one of them, and
  `𝔪 ⊆ 𝔪²` gives `𝔪 = 0` by Nakayama, while `𝔪 ∈ Ass R` gives a nonzero `y` with `𝔪 y = 0` and
  hence, by the previous item, a free residue field, so that `R` is a field and `𝔪 = 0` again.
* `hasProjectiveDimensionLE_residueField_quotient` exhibits the residue field of `R/xR` as a
  direct summand of `𝔪/x𝔪` for `x ∈ 𝔪 ∖ 𝔪²`. The splitting is written down rather than obtained
  from a complementary system of generators: `x` spans a copy of `κ` inside `𝔪/x𝔪`, and the
  retraction onto it is `𝔪 → 𝔪/𝔪² → κ → κ(R/xR)`, the middle map a `κ`-functional carrying the
  image of `x` in the cotangent space to `1`, which exists because `x ∉ 𝔪²` makes that image
  nonzero. Such a composite kills `x𝔪` automatically, so it descends to `𝔪/x𝔪`, and a retract
  has no larger projective dimension (`CategoryTheory.Retract.hasProjectiveDimensionLT`).

`isPrincipal_of_free`, that a free ideal of a commutative ring is principal, is the general
lemma behind the case `d = 1`; it is stated for an arbitrary commutative ring, with neither a
local nor a Noetherian hypothesis, since neither is used.

## References

* [StacksProject 00OA](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00OA)
-/

